# Challenge137 (NewScenario22 topology) — VirtualBox Vagrantfile
#
# Box swapped generic/ubuntu2204 -> bento/ubuntu-22.04, same version
# PROVISIONING already runs (202401.31.0). Validated end-to-end on
# Challenge130 first (same non-uniform mesh shape, router-1 hub with r2r
# degree 4, RIP, persist_boot_commands-based LAN/gateway addressing,
# network/traffic — all confirmed working including full reboot-survival
# testing), then rolled out to Challenge70 and Challenge101 before this one.
# This scenario's topology files were already clean going in — gateway
# already split into its own file and correctly ordered last, both
# providers already present, no dst_port gap in its traffic flow, no
# no double-curly-brace landmine comments — so the box swap is the only change
# needed here.
#
# NOTE: router-1 has r2r degree 4 (6 total adapters: NAT + mgmt + 4 r2r
# links) — same PCI-bridge slot overflow trigger seen on Challenge101/
# Challenge78/Challenge130, and confirmed to reproduce identically on bento
# (Challenge130's router-1 hit the exact same guest-eth-naming scramble).
# Not a new risk from this swap: network/resolve_iface_roles +
# fix_router_netplan.py already resolve it automatically via MAC-based
# reassignment (confirmed working on bento, zero code changes needed). If
# router-1 is unreachable via plain `ssh` while `vagrant ssh router-1`
# still works, that's this exact signature — check
# /etc/goad/iface-roles.env on router-1 rather than reaching for a manual
# fix.
#
# :networks order on each router MUST match that router's r2r_interfaces
# key order in ../../data/config.json — both are hand-maintained, nothing
# derives one from the other automatically. Verified against config.json
# at delivery time:
#   router-1: router-4, router-3, router-5, router-2
#   router-2: router-4, router-1
#   router-3: router-1, router-5
#   router-4: router-2, router-1
#   router-5: router-1, router-3

